<Base
  title="Business Plan"
  description="The Leaf & Lantern business plan: concept, market analysis, operations, financial model, brand system, and development roadmap."
>
  <div class="progress-track" aria-hidden="true"><div class="progress-bar" id="progress"></div></div>

  <header class="plan-hero">
    <div class="container plan-hero-inner">
      <span class="plan-kicker">Concept & Feasibility Plan · Prepared 2026</span>
      <h1>Leaf <em>&</em> Lantern</h1>
      <p class="plan-subtitle">An April-to-December Seasonal Gathering Place in Southeast Michigan</p>
      <p class="plan-thesis">
        Destination emotion. Everyday operating discipline. Built for return visits.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="container plan-layout">
    <nav class="plan-toc" aria-label="Plan sections">
      <span class="toc-title">Contents</span>
      <ol>
        {toc.map((t) => (
          <li><a href={`#${t.id}`} data-toc={t.id}>{t.label}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <a class="btn btn-primary toc-cta" href={url('/')}>View the Concept Site</a>
    </nav>

    <article class="plan-body">
      <!-- ============ Executive Summary ============ -->
      <section id="summary" class="plan-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">01 · Executive Summary</span>
        <h2>A viable concept. A permanent site that still has to earn its capital.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Leaf & Lantern converts the emotional pull of a Michigan cider mill into repeat
          visitation: exceptional cider, donuts, and coffee, fast human service, a warm
          courtyard, one flexible program hall, and curated retail. The concept is credible —
          the market is there, customer prices are mostly grounded, and the operating strategy
          deliberately strips out the things that usually destroy destination-food businesses.
          The open question is capital and volume: whether the business can reach roughly
          <strong>85–90k annual food transactions</strong> without requiring a <strong>$4M+
          operating asset</strong> to do it.
        </p>
        <p>
          To customers, the strategy reads as <strong>“More Than Great Cider and
          Donuts.”</strong> Economically the model is a dual engine:
          <strong>hero food drives visitation; standardized programming monetizes the property
          across the rest of the calendar.</strong> Retail, education, and programs are not
          ancillary upside — they are necessary. What remains excluded is the failure mode:
          weddings, banquets, attraction parks, and bespoke private events.
        </p>
        <p>
          The investment posture is <strong>pilots first</strong>. Fifty-five thousand Year-1
          food transactions remains the management target; the underwriting case banks
          40–45k. A permanent-site commitment waits on measured demand, school and camp
          presales, demonstrated maker fees, and a site/building combination that clears the
          ~$4M all-in capital gate — preferably through adaptive reuse.
        </p>

        <div class="metric-grid">
          {metrics.map((m) => (
            <div class="metric-card">
              <span class="metric-value">{m.value}</span>
              <span class="metric-label">{m.label}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <blockquote class="pull-quote">Autumn is our heart, not our entire calendar.</blockquote>

        <p>
          The property operates <strong>April through December</strong> in four seasonal chapters —
          Discovery, Gathering, Tradition, and Celebration. January through March, it closes to
          the public for maintenance, planning, and hiring.
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Season</th><th>Signature experience</th><th>Primary promise</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Spring</strong></td><td>Discovery</td><td>Nature, schools, gardens, and hands-on learning</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Summer</strong></td><td>Gathering</td><td>Camps, markets, music, wellness, and long evenings</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Autumn</strong></td><td>Tradition</td><td>Cider, donuts, coffee, pumpkins, and Halloween</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Holiday</strong></td><td>Celebration</td><td>Gift markets, lights, workshops, and warm drinks</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          The opening footprint is deliberately lean: one adaptable main building of roughly
          3,500–5,500 square feet, plus a courtyard, a donut line and beverage counter, a curated
          market, outdoor teaching areas, and right-sized parking. The plan proceeds only after
          four gates are passed — zoning and utility feasibility, a bottom-up 2030-dollar
          construction estimate including land, demand validation through pop-ups and advance
          bookings, and confirmation that peak-season throughput supports the brand experience
          without a $4M+ fragile fixed-cost base.
        </p>
        <p class="scope-note">
          This document is a concept and feasibility plan with a conservative underwriting lens.
          A subsequent investment memorandum — prepared after the pilot seasons — will add team
          biographies, ownership structure, capital sought, use of proceeds, levered IRR,
          terminal-value assumptions, replacement-capex reserves, and a downside sale scenario.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Concept & Mission ============ -->
      <section id="concept" class="plan-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">02 · Concept & Mission</span>
        <h2>A real place with a purpose in every operating season.</h2>
        <p>
          Leaf & Lantern is an April-to-December seasonal gathering place and food business
          focused on family traditions, local food, creative learning, community gatherings, and
          Michigan-made products. It does not need to operate as a large production farm, but its
          offerings remain materially connected to seasons, food, growing, making, and the
          landscape — never a generic rental hall temporarily decorated for different holidays.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          Leaf & Lantern creates warm, seasonal experiences that help people slow down, spend
          time together, and enjoy the traditions, food, creativity, and natural character of
          Michigan.
        </blockquote>

        <BrandImage
          src="/images/entrance-autumn.jpg"
          alt="The Leaf & Lantern arrival experience: cream hanging sign, stone bases, warm lanterns"
          ratio="21 / 9"
          class="plan-figure"
        />
        <p class="figure-note">
          Representative concept imagery; final architecture, signage, and branding may differ.
        </p>

        <h3>Operating principles</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Business implication</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Seasonally defined</strong></td><td>Every event fits Discovery, Gathering, Tradition, or Celebration.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Intimate rather than crowded</strong></td><td>Timed capacity and comfortable circulation take precedence over maximum attendance.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Food is central</strong></td><td>Cider, donuts, and coffee drive visitation; programming is the necessary second engine that monetizes the calendar — never bespoke events.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Flexible rather than sprawling</strong></td><td>One excellent adaptable building serves camps, yoga, markets, food service, workshops, seminars, and daytime retreats — never weddings or banquets.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Local but edited</strong></td><td>Makers and products are selected for quality, utility, and fit.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Warm, not kitschy</strong></td><td>Natural materials, light, landscape, and real products rather than excessive props.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Professional, not corporate</strong></td><td>Operations, booking, food safety, and service stay disciplined even when the atmosphere is relaxed.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The recommended structure is a for-profit operating company with the option of a
          separate property-holding entity. Educational partnerships may later justify a nonprofit
          partner, but the core business does not depend on grants to sustain ordinary operations.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Market ============ -->
      <section id="market" class="plan-section">
        <span class="eyebrow">03 · Market & Positioning</span>
        <h2>Familiar territory. A different operating philosophy.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The primary market is defined by drive time, not county lines: 30-, 45-, and 60-minute
          bands centered on the eventual parcel in the Northville–South Lyon–Farmington corridor,
          weighted toward western Wayne County, southern and western Oakland County, and eastern
          Livingston County.
        </p>
        <p>
          For context, the broader tri-county region (Wayne, Oakland, Macomb) held approximately
