        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          Hero food drives visitation; standardized programming monetizes the property across
          the rest of the calendar.
        </blockquote>

        <h3>Hero products, sold many ways</h3>
        <p>
          The autumn menu is a production system of roughly a dozen purchase buttons: donuts
          (single, half-dozen, dozen), cider (cup, half-gallon, gallon), a $2 Morning Cup of
          locally roasted batch coffee, a double espresso, an Americano, hot chocolate, a $2
          dog donut, two family bundles, and one rotating item — no milk-steaming matrix, syrup
          ladder, or size tree. A staff-operated superautomatic espresso machine keeps the
          coffee automated behind the counter while the service stays human in front of it.
          This is not competing with bakeries: a few singular products, made consistently, with
          secondary pastries curated from partners. Item names carry the same discipline as the
          item count: <strong>“Morning Cup — $2,”</strong> not “$2 coffee”; <strong>“Fresh
          Donut,”</strong> not “Cinnamon Sugar Ring.” Plain, confident names remove decision
          fatigue and read as intentional rather than cheap.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="ll-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Ordering lane</th><th>Best use</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {lanes.map((l) => (
                <tr><td><strong>{l.lane}</strong></td><td>{l.use}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Order-ahead runs on a managed commerce platform (Square, Toast, or Lightspeed class)
          rather than custom software, on one price list everywhere — walk-up, mobile, and
          drive-up — so no one shops for a better rate while standing in line. Every order is
          handed over at a dedicated staffed pickup counter, physically separate from the
          walk-up queue: no lockers, no customer-facing kiosks. The guest’s incentive is the
          workflow — a kitchen pickup window and food ready on arrival; the kitchen’s payoff is
          batching to known demand, with preorders targeted at 15–25% of peak sales. Pickup
          windows belong to the kitchen, not to admission: they organize handoff timing without
          reserving a place on the grounds. Office and school bulk orders follow the Yates
          mechanic — minimum quantities, 48-hour lead time, volume pricing.
        </p>

        <h3>A gateway product, not a loss leader</h3>
        <p>
          The $2 Morning Cup is easy to misread as a loss leader, and the distinction matters.
          A Costco hot dog exists to justify a membership; IKEA’s café exists to increase dwell
          time; McDonald’s Dollar Menu historically filled slow periods. Leaf & Lantern’s
          problem is different — not getting a guest to spend $2, but getting a parent on the
          school run, a commuter, someone collecting a dozen for the office, or someone walking
          the grounds to think, <em>“Sure, let’s stop at Leaf & Lantern.”</em> If the coffee is
          locally roasted, simple, always good, and always hot, the Morning Cup is not
          competing with Starbucks — Starbucks is five minutes away. It removes hesitation, and
          it makes a statement not about margin but about the brand: <strong>we are not trying
          to maximize every transaction.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          The hero product gets the opposite treatment. Pricing a fresh cider-mill donut too
          low sends the wrong signal: destination donut shops currently run roughly $2.40–$2.75
          for a standard donut, with premium donuts substantially higher. The ladder is
          therefore a single Fresh Donut at $3, a half-dozen at modest per-donut savings, and a
          dozen at meaningful savings — so the arithmetic itself says <em>buy a box</em>. The
          dog donut is carried for emotional value, not margin: near-zero food cost when made
          intelligently from dog-safe trimmings, endlessly photographed, and a quiet signal
          that the dog is part of the visit.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          No complicated menu. Just the things we think should be done exceptionally well.
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          The result is deliberately not a value brand — it is a brand where every purchase
          feels easy to justify. A family that leaves having spent $28 on donuts (a dozen plus
          one for the dog), $12 on a half-gallon of cider, and $4 on two Morning Cups should
          think, <em>“That was absolutely worth it.”</em> The psychology being optimized is
          obvious, fair choices — not the lowest prices.
        </p>

