        <h3>Break-even definition</h3>
        <p>
          Cash break-even is the level at which operating cash flow covers all operating
          expenses — including market-rate owner-manager compensation within payroll — plus
          interest and scheduled principal repayment. Under the corrected stack
          (2030 labor, land, escalation, normalized maker fees), that level moves from the
          prior ≈70,000 food transactions to roughly <strong>80–85,000</strong>, and potentially
          ≈90,000 if land or construction run hot. Illustratively, financing 65% of a
          capital-gated ≈$4.0M all-in project at 7% over twenty years requires roughly $235K of
          annual debt service. Income taxes apply only above break-even; depreciation is
          excluded as a non-cash item. Ground-lease alternatives replace land debt with
          occupancy cost and will be modeled at site selection.
        </p>

        <h3>Investor returns <small>(illustrative path — formal returns in the investment memorandum)</small></h3>
